WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:10.300
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Hi, I'm Kelsey
Hightower, a staff developer

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.254
advocate for Google
Cloud Platform.

00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:14.170
ARMON DADGAR: And I'm
Armon Dadgar, co-founder

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.189
and CTO of HashiCorp.

00:00:15.189 --> 00:00:17.480
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: This is
the first video in our series

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.420
covering open-source
tools from HashiCorp

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.791
and how we can use them
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:21.791 --> 00:00:23.290
In the next few
videos, you're going

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.220
to learn about using Vagrant,
Packer, and Terraform with GCP.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.220
Armon, can you briefly
explain those videos?

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.620
ARMON DADGAR: Sure.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.370
You know, Vagrant is
a pretty popular tool

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:34.570
that many people
have maybe played

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:35.653
with or at least heard of.

00:00:35.653 --> 00:00:38.530
And it's commonly used to bring
up a development environment

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.810
in a quick, repeatable manner.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.850
Packer falls sort
of right after that,

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.779
helping people to build
those machine images

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.320
and build those sort
of environments,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.050
either for development, so
build images for Vagrant,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.060
or to build
production-oriented images,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.604
for example, a GCP, a VM image.

00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:56.020
And then Terraform
is kind of next

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.270
on the kind of
development-to-production

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.790
lifecycle, and it helps people
provision infrastructure,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.250
whether that's a
vSphere cluster locally

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.302
or a GCP cluster in the cloud.

00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:06.760
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
So if I'm a user,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.051
I'm hearing all of these tools.

00:01:08.051 --> 00:01:09.750
I'm like, that's a
lot of puzzle pieces.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.130
Can you touch on how you tie
all of these tools together?

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:12.850
ARMON DADGAR: Sure.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.391
I think this really
goes back to kind

00:01:14.391 --> 00:01:17.880
of our mission at HashiCorp and
why the company even exists.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.630
When we started the
company, it was really

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.790
to look at that
application delivery

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.250
process from development
through to production,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.260
and say, like, what are those
pieces that you really need,

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.860
the necessary and
sufficient components?

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.680
And then building an open-source
tool to do each of those things

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.660
so that you can
provision, secure, and run

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.310
any type of infrastructure
for any type of application.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.393
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:01:38.393 --> 00:01:41.040
And users who I know have
played with HashiCorp tools,

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.540
they all tell me it's like all
the tools have a similar look

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:44.320
and feel.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.956
Is there a common approach you
take to building these tools?

00:01:46.956 --> 00:01:48.580
ARMON DADGAR: Yeah,
I'm glad you asked.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:49.996
There's a document
we've published

00:01:49.996 --> 00:01:52.300
called the "Tao of HashiCorp,"
where we talk about what

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.570
the design principles and
kind of the core philosophy

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.090
is behind kind of our
approach to infrastructure,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.880
as well as our approach to
actually building tools.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.190
And that comes across
in kind of the look

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.910
and feel of the
tools themselves.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:05.290
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
So I think that's

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.150
one thing that sets
HashiCorp apart,

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.039
is how this look and
feel of the tools are.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:10.360
Can you talk about
some of the principles

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.549
behind that look and feel?

00:02:11.549 --> 00:02:12.340
ARMON DADGAR: Sure.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.002
We outline all of
them in the "Tao."

00:02:14.002 --> 00:02:15.460
There's maybe six
or seven of them.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.920
But I think the three that
are kind of the most important

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.530
for people is a
focus on workflows,

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.860
infrastructure as
code, and immutability.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.570
The workflow focus for us
is very, very important,

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.030
because our view is that
users are going to bring

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.200
their own technology, right?

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.240
So some users,
they're going to be

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.771
living in a world of
vSphere and on-premise,

00:02:33.771 --> 00:02:35.020
and how do we meet them there?

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.580
And other users are
going to be fully

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.620
in the cloud, running
everything in GCP,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.910
and how do we support that?

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.666
And other people are going to
cross this entire spectrum.

00:02:42.666 --> 00:02:44.290
So that's sort of
our focus on workflow

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.670
as opposed to technology.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.460
And then when we get to
immutability, the idea is--

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.920
how do we avoid changing our
applications, our machines,

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.319
our infrastructure in place,
where we might introduce errors

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:56.860
into production and
then cause things

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.990
to fail in sort of
unexpected ways,

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.840
and instead build brand-new
versions that we fully know

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.262
and understand and can vet?

00:03:03.262 --> 00:03:05.470
And I think then the third
kind of most important one

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.900
is this notion of
infrastructure as code, which

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.900
is another technique to kind
of manage the complexity

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.492
and make sure we can go to
large scale in a sane way.

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:15.450
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So I
have a lot of experience

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.510
with infrastructure as code.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.570
But maybe for some of the
people out there watching,

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.900
could you give me a
little bit more detail

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.550
about what
infrastructure as code is

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.590
and what it means to HashiCorp?

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.820
ARMON DADGAR: Yeah.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.820
I think it's a
pretty general idea.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.360
It can apply to all
sorts of things.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.730
But I think the kernel
of it is this notion

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.670
that what we're used to is
having sort of in our heads

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.010
the idea of how our development
environment is built,

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.774
how our clusters are managed.

00:03:37.774 --> 00:03:39.190
And what we're
really trying to do

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.200
is take that that's
locked away in our head,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.000
and bring it in and
have it live as code

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.250
that actually can be executed.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:44.749
Right?

00:03:44.749 --> 00:03:46.630
And this way we can
version control it.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:47.530
We can reuse it.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.130
We can share it
with one another.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.350
We can extend it over time.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.060
And we can really begin to apply
the best practices of software

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.060
development to infrastructure
management, right?

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.400
And so the goal is
to kind of get away

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.790
from the oral tradition where
you're like, Bob in the corner

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.470
really knows how everything
works, and nobody else does.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.510
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: All
right, so let's be honest.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.550
Is this technique
unique to Terraform?

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.425
Or do you see this
technique in other places?

00:04:08.425 --> 00:04:10.508
ARMON DADGAR: So it's a
bit loaded, because I know

00:04:10.508 --> 00:04:12.490
you used to work at Puppet.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:15.230
But you know, I think to give
credit where credit is due,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.800
I think this idea was really
first pioneered by the config

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.146
management community,
where they really

00:04:20.146 --> 00:04:22.270
realized that it's getting
increasingly complicated

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:23.353
to manage a modern server.

00:04:23.353 --> 00:04:26.080
So just understanding users
and packages and files

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.850
and directories and
permissions, blah, is too much.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.670
So how do we have all
of that live as code

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.280
and be able to
manage our servers

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.390
in this programmatic way?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:37.360
So I think the config management
community really pioneered it,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.220
and now we've taken
that and are applying it

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.797
to sort of the broader
HashiCorp tool chain.

00:04:41.797 --> 00:04:42.880
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.640
So you talked about
this focus on workflows.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.047
How does that focus on workflows
affect HashiCorp and GCP?

00:04:50.047 --> 00:04:51.880
ARMON DADGAR: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.380
Like I said, for
us it's this belief

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.020
that we have to meet our
users where they are, in terms

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.490
of their technology choices.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.500
And for us, a huge
part of our community

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.570
lives and operates
and consumes GCP.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.180
So for us, it's really important
to make sure with Vagrant,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.040
you can have a
development box on GCP.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.870
With Packer, you can
build images for it.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.860
Terraform, you can
manage your GCP clusters,

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:10.980
so on and so forth.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.870
It's that all of the tool chains
should integrate with GCP,

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.120
because that's a technology
choice users want to make.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:16.640
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
All right, so hey.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.500
Thanks, Armon, for
joining me today.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.840
How can more people learn
about more of HashiCorp tools

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.800
and the philosophy
behind HashiCorp?

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:23.670
ARMON DADGAR: Yeah,
thanks for having me.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.961
It's always a pleasure, Kelsey.

00:05:24.961 --> 00:05:27.470
I'd recommend people visit
our homepage, HashiCorp.com,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.110
to learn more about the
company and the tools.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.870
Or alternatively, people can
just watch your talk on YouTube

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.140
that you gave at Hashi-Con
about delivering a 12-factor app

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.016
with the Hashi stack.

00:05:37.016 --> 00:05:37.973
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Cool.

00:05:37.973 --> 00:05:39.600
So hey, thanks
